### Manual requests for the ComputeSeparation Aspire harness.
### The fake platform sends the platform requests: it assigns the pod on the WorkerProxy, assigns the placeholder Host
### to the app, and links the worker to the Host.
### Its /simulate routes are harness-only; the WorkerProxy and Host routes they call are the real contracts.
###
### Run AppHost with the `project-placeholder-manual` launch profile (F5, or the command below). It pins the ports used
### here and turns off automatic assign and link so you can send each step yourself, in order.
###
###   dotnet run --project tools\ComputeSeparation\AppHost\ComputeSeparation.AppHost.csproj --launch-profile project-placeholder-manual

@platform = http://localhost:7060
@host = http://127.0.0.1:7071
@workerProxy = http://localhost:7081

### Expected flow, in order.

### 1. The platform assigns the pod on the WorkerProxy. Expect 201, or 204 if repeated.
### 503 WorkerNotReady means the sample worker has not connected yet; retry.
POST {{platform}}/simulate/worker/assign

### Optional: the WorkerProxy pod state is now ReadyForRequest.
GET {{workerProxy}}/admin/worker/state

### 2. The platform assigns the placeholder Host to the app (POST /admin/instance/assign). Expect 202, or 409 if
### repeated. The Host finishes specializing only after step 3 links the worker.
POST {{platform}}/simulate/host/assign

### 3. The platform links the worker to the Host. Expect 201, or 200 for a matching retry.
POST {{platform}}/simulate/worker/link

### 4. A client invokes the function through Host -> WorkerProxy -> worker.
GET {{host}}/api/hello
